  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nMostly sunny conditions across the Florida Peninsula as of early \nthis afternoon. The atmosphere is dry with this mornings 12z \nsounding indicating 0.94 inches of PW. These PW values are down \naround the 10th percentile of climatology for mid-October. There is \nstill a small chance for a few showers offshore and across far \nsoutheastern portions of the forecast area where the moisture is a \nlittle bit higher. \n\nIn the upper levels, there is a longwave trough that extends from \nthe Mid-Atlantic southward into the western Caribbean. At the \nsurface, high pressure over the northeastern U.S. is leading to \nnortheasterly winds across the region. There are some fluctuations to \nthe upper air and surface patterns but overall conditions remain \nrelatively similar into early next week. \n\nThe northeasterly winds will be the main impact throughout the \nforecast period. Northeasterly winds are already elevated across the \narea. These winds really look to increase over the weekend as the \nsurface high to the northeast drifts to the south. This will lead to \nan increase in the pressure gradient across the region and \nincreasing winds. Wind Advisories may be necessary, at least for the \ncoast. These windy conditions will continue into early next week. \n\nThese strong winds will also occur with higher than normal tides and \nproduce additional hazard with beach erosion, rough surf, minor \ncoastal flooding, and dangerous rip current. \n\nHighs will generally be in the 70s and 80s throughout the forecast \nperiod with lows generally in the 60s. Dry conditions will feature \nthroughout much of the forecast period except for over the weekend. \nPoPs increase to around 50-60% mainly along the coast.",
